WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:02.880
What are you doing tonight?

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:03.700
In fact,

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.600
everybody watching: what are you all doing tonight?

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.520
Watching some movies, maybe? Gonna see some TV?

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.160
Scroll, scroll through the infinite scroll on Tumblr, maybe?

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.380
Or on Facebook? Maybe you're gonna catch up a little on work?

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.740
Read a book? Do some errands?

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:15.480
No.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.780
I'm not going to let you do any of those things.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.920
Instead, we -- all of us -- we're gonna do a thing together.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:20.980
And no exceptions.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.120
We are all going to write a letter to someone who represents us in government.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.440
It's an assignment! You're watching a video, and in return, you're getting homework, basically!

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.940
If you don't want to write a letter, put a stamp on it and put it in the mail,

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.060
stop watching this video right now, cuz I'm gonna make you feel really guilty if you don't do it.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.340
Unless, you live in a country that doesn't have democracy.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.980
In which case, it is your responsibility to make all of us who do live in democracies feel real guilty for not sending letters.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.820
So, I'm gonna talk about how to send a good letter to your government representative or someone who works in your government.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.800
I'm actually writing one of my letters by hand. Now, of course, this is not necessary, but I'm doing this because

00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:00.240
I know that this is all about time capital -- it's about value -- and sending an email is easy,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.740
while writing a letter by hand, and hand-delivering it to the person's office, that's hard.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.140
That's more likely to get noticed, and the nutty thing is these letters actually *matter*.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:14.140
I actually had a hard time believing this, but I have talked to a lot of staffers from congressional offices and they agree:

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:20.880
letters matter and phone calls matter -- and they matter more than Twitter and email because they show that the consituent cares more.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.020
A letter to a goverment representative should be respectful -- no curse words, no anger.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.460
It should outline who you are, and that you are part of the community that that person represents.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.400
You can outline /how/ you are a part of that community if you wish.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.720
It should also get to the point really fast. If there's a way that this particular issue is affecting you in particular,

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.040
or you have a personal story, you should share that, but do it quickly, and without griping.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:49.420
Outline -- ideally somehow separated a little bit from the rest of the letter -- what exactly you are requesting.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.600
I'm asking for a ban on assault rifles in America

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:58.120
and for Congress to allow the CDC to study and fund the study of the public health effects of guns after a 20-year ban.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:03.000
It's important to understand this particular representative's perspectives on the issue that you care about

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.840
so you know whether to thank them for their positions or not.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.800
I've put some info in the description for how to find goverment representatives and how to look up their voting records.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.400
Remember to include your address on the envelope in case they want to write you back,

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.340
which, remarkably, they often do.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:18.740
I actually wrote three letters: one to each of my senators and one to my representative,

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:22.980
the text of which can be found in the description. Feel free to pull from those or to be inspired by them,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.600
but, of course, make them different.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.700
Form letters are noticed immediately by staffers not given the same weight.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:32.860
Of course, your issue can be whatever you want your issue to be, but I want /us/,

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:37.080
as a community, to reach out to our governments -- whatever country you're in --

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.120
and let them know that we care about our countries.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.300
After watching Hanna's video this week, I simply could not not do this.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.740
Right now, or tonight, but in the next 24 hours, do this thing.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:53.180
Do it for me, do it for you, do it for your country, do it for whatever you want to do it for.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.640
When we confine our shouting to the internet, to Facebook, and Twitter and Tumblr, and talking to other people who agree with us --

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:04.280
we might make ourselves feel better and feel righteous, but we are not on the path that creates the most change.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.220
So one day, for one hour, instead of doing whatever you were gonna do tonight,

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.000
do this thing.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.540
If you've written your letter, if you want to share it, you can send it to me on Snapchat or Twitter or Facebook or Tumblr.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.140
You can use a hashtag or a tag if you you're on a place that supports those things,

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.820
so you can see other people's letters, and if you see them, thank them for sending their letter.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.840
Thank you for doing this, I found it empowering,

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.940
and a little scary,

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.540
but if you're not doing something that scares you every once in a while, you're doing it wrong.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.440
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

